WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.170
After a gamma bomb exploded in his face, Bruce
Banner gained incredible powers that turned

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.220
him into a colossal green superhero.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.690
Cursed with serious self-control issues and
a major case of self-loathing, Hulk became

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:16.509
one of the most intriguing characters in Marvel's
pantheon — and in the past fifty years,

00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:19.990
Marvel has adapted the Emerald Avenger countless
times for the screen.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.070
Where do all those Hulks rank?

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.269
Here are the highs and lows of his smashing
career.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:25.269
"So!

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:28.419
This all seems… horrible."

00:00:28.419 --> 00:00:32.090
Travis Willingham in Super Hero Squad

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:36.360
In 2009, The Super Hero Squad Show featured
adaptations of classic Marvel storylines set

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.040
in Superhero City — overseen by Mayor Stan
Lee, of course.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:44.200
Travis Willingham provided the voice of the
show's Hulk, and although the Green Goliath

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.830
was a regular player, the series kept the
stories simple; Hulk mostly made juvenile

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.500
jabs at his teammates and pulverized stuff.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.480
And while the super-fan gags were entertaining
enough for an adult audience, the real goal

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.490
of the series was almost certainly selling
toys to impressionable children.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.680
"Don't be scared, plant.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.880
Scary claw have ISSUES."

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.540
Eric Bana in Hulk

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.440
You probably know Ang Lee's 2003 film for
this beautifully shot sequence, which follows

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.830
Hulk as he hops from mountaintop to mountaintop
in the ruddy desert.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:20.760
Sadly, Lee's entry was an otherwise clunky
affair, full of tacked-on nonsense like a

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.080
CGI brawl between old Purple Pants and some
monster dogs.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.810
And while Eric Bana's performance is a classic
right down to that signature line —

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.560
"You're making me angry."

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.170
— we can't say the same for the CGI.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:36.530
A decade later, this Hulk looks dated and
cumbersome.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.680
Ron Perlman in The Marvel Action Hour

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.380
Technically, Ron Perlman only played the Hulk
in two episodes of the Marvel Action Hour

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.680
— but come on, it's Ron friggin' Perlman!

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.560
We'd never leave him off this list.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.500
Perlman really threw himself into the role,
but since his legacy as the Hulk is hampered

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.940
by his limited screentime, most people will
remember him for the way he looks in red,

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.780
not green.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.630
"Excuse me?"

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.420
Fred Tatasciore in Agents of SMASH

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:08.410
In 2013, the Hulk got his very own spinoff
in the animated series Agents of S.M.A.S.H.,

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:13.260
which stands for the Supreme Military Agency
of Super Humans, because of course it does.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.920
His team of bruisers included She-Hulk, his
hulky son Skaar, the Red Hulk, and Rick Jones

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.760
as his gamma-irradiated alter-ego, A-Bomb.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.439
The series was short-lived, but showcased
some solid Hulk action and a dollop of personal

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.800
development, plus the voice talents of actor
Fred Tatasciore.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.000
"Ahhh!"

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.810
Edward Norton in The Incredible Hulk

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.650
Five years after the character's first big-screen
adventure, the franchise rebooted itself with

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:43.159
The Incredible Hulk, the movie that introduced
the character to the still-new Marvel Cinematic

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:44.159
Universe.

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:48.591
Edward Norton gave audiences a nuanced Bruce
Banner, and even the motion-captured CG Hulk

00:02:48.591 --> 00:02:49.659
looks good.

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:53.930
Unfortunately, creative tensions between Marvel
and Norton, who rewrote the script, forced

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.140
the actor to make his own Hulk-sized exit
from the franchise.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.340
"Raghhh!"

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.730
Michael Bell and Bob Holt in The Incredible
Hulk and The Amazing Spider-Man

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:12.169
In 1982, Spider Man and His Amazing Friends
merged with The Incredible Hulk to create

00:03:12.169 --> 00:03:13.730
an '80s power hour.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.019
Michael Bell voiced Bruce Banner, while Bob
Holt — who also voiced the Grape Ape — provided

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:19.370
the Hulk's snarls.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.930
Like its predecessors, this series centered
on Banner's episodic quest to cure himself,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.170
in a story that remained faithful to the Hulk's
bombed-out origins.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:31.959
The series also saw Hulk take on Quasimodo
in an epic feud that Marvel later adapted

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:33.239
into its own comic.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:36.819
Some of the plots were half-baked and the
animation could be choppy — but powered

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:40.960
by '80s nostalgia, this super-friend lands
pretty close to the top.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.500
"Rahhhh!

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.340
Ahhhh!"

00:03:44.909 --> 00:03:48.200
Neal McDonough and Lou Ferrigno in The Incredible
Hulk

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:53.839
In 1996, the Marvel greenlit this animated
series to the satisfaction of Hulk fans everywhere.

00:03:53.839 --> 00:03:57.709
Neal McDonough voiced Banner, while Lou Ferrigno
— who had already played the green giant

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:01.490
in the live-action '70s series — came full
circle as the voice of the Hulk.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.489
"Stop calling me Hulk - I'm a person, okay?"

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:09.040
This show was pretty solid, and wasn't just
a smash-a-thon, particularly during a downbeat

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.299
first season when Banner and Hulk both oozed
self-loathing.

00:04:12.299 --> 00:04:16.650
But the next season threw too many additional
Hulks into the mix and overburdened the dialogue

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.799
with one-liners, leaving the series with a
mere two-season lifespan.

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:24.169
Paul Soles and Max Ferguson in The Marvel
Super Heroes

00:04:24.169 --> 00:04:29.400
Let's go back in time to 1966, when The Marvel
Super Heroes explored the adventures of Iron

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.240
Man, Namor, Thor, and of course, the Hulk.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.729
Paul Soles and Max Ferguson lent their voices
to Bruce Banner and the Hulk and each mini-episode

00:04:37.729 --> 00:04:39.400
tackled one of his stories.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:44.020
The animation studio used an old-school technique
called xerography to capture the original

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.120
spirit of Marvel's greenest guardian.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.610
The visuals aren't impressive by modern-day
standards, but it's still got plenty of vintage

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:51.610
charm.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:57.850
"Doc Bruce Banner, belted by gamma rays turns
into the Hulk!

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.669
Ain't he unglamorous?"

00:04:59.669 --> 00:05:02.650
Bill Bixby and Lou Ferrigno in The Incredible
Hulk

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:07.669
Starring Bixby as David Banner and Ferrigno
as the Hulk, CBS' The Incredible Hulk delivered

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:12.430
four seasons of pants-shredding action between
1978 and 1982.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.919
The series worked over the source material,
changing Banner's first name and completely

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:19.229
ignoring his usual cast of sidekicks.

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:24.569
Instead, intrepid reporter Jack McGee trailed
Banner on his cross-country quest for a cure.

00:05:24.569 --> 00:05:29.129
The stunts, effects, and formulaic plots might
be corny by today's standards, but Hulk's

00:05:29.129 --> 00:05:33.379
writers treated Banner and his plight with
respect — and the show still inspires the

00:05:33.379 --> 00:05:36.729
many Hulks who've followed in Lou's chalky
green footsteps.

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:41.729
After CBS canceled the series in 1982, NBC
snatched up the rights and revived Banner

00:05:41.729 --> 00:05:46.690
and his big green alter-ego in three follow-up
TV movies — which included Stan Lee's first

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.620
live-action cameo… though obviously not
his last.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.520
"Wow."

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.090
Mark Ruffalo in The Avengers

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:00.430
Mark Ruffalo sets the standard for modern
era Hulks with his performances in The Avengers

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.880
and Age of Ultron, which perfectly suit the
tongue-in-cheek tone of the Marvel Cinematic

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:05.880
Universe.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.710
True, this Banner doesn't get much lab time
or even a proper origin story, but his intellectual

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:15.419
sparring with Tony Stark proves he's got some
serious brains — and while alien invasions

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:19.500
and robot overlords don't leave much room
for a personal life, those interludes with

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.819
Black Widow prove that he can smash walls
and break hearts.

00:06:22.819 --> 00:06:27.460
Sadly, we're still waiting on a proper MCU
solo adventure for Ruffalo's take on the big

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:28.460
green guy.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.889
But for now, he's a valuable team player and
the man behind many primo cinematic moments

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:37.889
— from the Hulkbuster fight in Avengers
2, to the thrashing Loki needs and deserves,

00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:41.449
to revealing the one weird trick to hulking
out on command.

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:46.180
"That's my secret, Captain - I'm always angry..."

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.200
Thanks for watching!

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.360
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.900
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

